\outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 Alexander\'92s ticket distribution continued well into the afternoon and reached people his marketing department would probably not have included in its ideal audience profile.\
The High Elvis accepted one.\
Halo accepted one.\
Gerald \'93Very\'94 Hardman accepted one too.\
None of them had quite the same reason for wanting to attend 
\f1\i Candid Criminal
\f0\i0 , but then neither did the rest of the audience.\
At half past four, Shakti Security opened the Sublime Point gates to ticket holders.\
The auditorium doors would not open for another hour, which left the early arrivals with a compound carpark, several hundred other people and nothing organised to do.\
The Elvi fixed that.\
It was difficult to determine exactly when part of the carpark became a stage. An amplifier appeared. Then another. Guitar cases accumulated. Someone found power. Musicians who had arrived to watch 
\f1\i Candid Criminal
\f0\i0  discovered that they had also arrived carrying everything necessary not to watch 
\f1\i Candid Criminal
\f0\i0  for at least another hour.\
Soon there were Elvi performing for Elvi who were queuing to watch other people perform for television.\
Shakti Security let it happen. They had tickets. They were invited guests. Most were carrying guitars, amplifiers or items that looked enough like musical equipment to make detailed investigation socially exhausting.\
Outside the gate, the UDF watched civilians walk into the compound it had surrounded.\
The gates being open did not mean the UDF was welcome through them. This distinction appeared perfectly straightforward to Shakti Security.\
It was less straightforward to the UDF.\
For the next hour, ticket holders arrived by car, on foot and in whatever vehicles had survived the journey through Leura. The carpark filled. The Elvi stage enlarged by methods nobody remembered authorising. People who had come early because they wanted good seats found themselves watching a free concert while waiting to enter a television show about judicial execution.\
Shortly before six, Gerry Hardman approached the gate again.\
This time he had company.\
PAPER\'92s CLOWN personnel had dressed themselves as Elvis tribute artists.\
The disguises were adequate individually. Collectively, there were problems. Trained clandestine operatives had a tendency to know where everyone around them was, walk with purpose and refrain from bumping into one another. Actual Elvi wandered, argued, stopped without warning, carried too much equipment and frequently changed direction because someone twenty metres away had begun playing a song they knew.\
Gerry spread his people out.\
The effect improved.\
At 5:48 they joined the arriving audience.\
By then Shakti Security had processed enough sequins, capes, jumpsuits, leather, sunglasses, pompadours, guitars, guitar cases and competing interpretations of Elvis Presley that another group of tribute artists possessed almost no distinguishing characteristics whatsoever.\
They passed through the gate.\
At almost exactly the same time, a truck arrived bearing the words:\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f2\b \cf0 \strokec2 CAKE, BAGEL & ROLL CATERING SERVICES
\f0\b0 \strokec2 \
in glittering paint.\
It was difficult to object to catering arriving at an event where a substantial crowd had been gathering for more than an hour, particularly when part of that crowd had spontaneously established a concert in the carpark.\
The truck carried guitar cases.\
This was even less remarkable.\
There were guitar cases everywhere.\
Some belonged to performers. Some belonged to people who hoped to become performers. Some belonged to people who had brought guitars because experience had taught them never to attend a gathering of Elvi without one.\
The cases in the CAKE, BAGEL & ROLL truck contained Gyges discs.\
The truck passed through the gates while Gerry and the PAPER CLOWNs were passing through on foot.\
Nobody fired a shot.\
Nobody cut a fence.\
Nobody stormed a checkpoint.\
By 5:51, Gerry Hardman and a contingent of disguised PAPER personnel were inside Sublime Point, the Gyges discs were inside Sublime Point, the carpark had acquired a live stage, and the gates remained open for anyone entitled to attend 
\f1\i Candid Criminal
\f0\i0 .\
The UDF remained outside.\
They did not have tickets.\
